  "Question": "The expression\n\n\\[ \\frac{P+Q}{P-Q}-\\frac{P-Q}{P+Q}, \\]\n\nwhere \\(P=x+y\\) and \\(Q=x-y,\\) is equivalent to:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{x^2-y^2}{xy}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{x^2-y^2}{2xy}\\)",
    "C": "\\(1\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{xy}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{2xy}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The sums and differences of \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) simplify immediately:\n\\[\nP+Q=(x+y)+(x-y)=2x,\n\\]\n\\[\nP-Q=(x+y)-(x-y)=2y.\n\\]\nThe original denominators must be nonzero, so \\(x\\ne0\\) and \\(y\\ne0\\). On this domain, substitute and then use the common denominator \\(xy\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{P+Q}{P-Q}-\\frac{P-Q}{P+Q}\n&=\\frac{2x}{2y}-\\frac{2y}{2x}\\\\\n&=\\frac{x}{y}-\\frac{y}{x}\\\\\n&=\\frac{x^2-y^2}{xy}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus the equivalent expression is \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{x^2-y^2}{xy}}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
